                        "content": "     1\u2192# Options\n     2\u2192\n     3\u2192This document lists the options that linkding can be configured with and explains how to use them in the individual install scenarios.\n     4\u2192\n     5\u2192## Using options\n     6\u2192\n     7\u2192### Docker\n     8\u2192\n     9\u2192Options are passed as environment variables to the Docker container by using the `-e` argument when using `docker run`. For example:\n    10\u2192\n    11\u2192```\n    12\u2192docker run --name linkding -p 9090:9090 -d -e LD_DISABLE_URL_VALIDATION=True sissbruecker/linkding:latest\n    13\u2192```\n    14\u2192\n    15\u2192For multiple options, use one `-e` argument per option.\n    16\u2192\n    17\u2192### Docker-compose\n    18\u2192\n    19\u2192For docker-compose options are configured using an `.env` file. \n    20\u2192Follow the docker-compose setup in the README and copy `.env.sample` to `.env`. Then modify the options in `.env`.\n    21\u2192\n    22\u2192### Manual setup\n    23\u2192\n    24\u2192All options need to be defined as environment variables in the environment that linkding runs in.\n    25\u2192\n    26\u2192## List of options\n    27\u2192\n    28\u2192### `LD_SUPERUSER_NAME`\n    29\u2192\n    30\u2192Values: `String` | Default = None\n    31\u2192\n    32\u2192When set, creates an initial superuser with the specified username when starting the container.\n    33\u2192Does nothing if the user already exists.\n    34\u2192\n    35\u2192See [`LD_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD`](#ld_superuser_password) on how to configure the respective password.\n    36\u2192\n    37\u2192### `LD_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD`\n    38\u2192\n    39\u2192Values: `String` | Default = None\n    40\u2192\n    41\u2192The password for the initial superuser.\n    42\u2192When left undefined, the superuser will be created without a usable password, which means the user can not authenticate using credentials / through the login form, and can only be authenticated using proxy authentication (see [`LD_ENABLE_AUTH_PROXY`](#ld_enable_auth_proxy)).\n    43\u2192\n    44\u2192### `LD_DISABLE_BACKGROUND_TASKS`\n    45\u2192\n    46\u2192Values: `True`, `False` | Default = `False`\n    47\u2192\n    48\u2192Disables background tasks, such as creating snapshots for bookmarks on the [the Internet Archive Wayback Machine](https://archive.org/web/).\n    49\u2192Enabling this flag will prevent the background task processor from starting up, and prevents scheduling tasks.\n    50\u2192This might be useful if you are experiencing performance issues or other problematic behaviour due to background task processing.\n    51\u2192\n    52\u2192### `LD_DISABLE_URL_VALIDATION`\n    53\u2192\n    54\u2192Values: `True`, `False` | Default = `False`\n    55\u2192\n    56\u2192Completely disables URL validation for bookmarks.\n    57\u2192This can be useful if you intend to store non fully qualified domain name URLs, such as network paths, or you want to store URLs that use another protocol than `http` or `https`.\n    58\u2192\n    59\u2192### `LD_REQUEST_TIMEOUT`\n    60\u2192\n    61\u2192Values: `Integer` as seconds | Default = `60`\n    62\u2192\n    63\u2192Configures the request timeout in the uwsgi application server. This can be useful if you want to import a bookmark file with a high number of bookmarks and run into request timeouts.\n    64\u2192\n    65\u2192### `LD_SERVER_PORT`\n    66\u2192\n    67\u2192Values: Valid port number | Default = `9090`\n    68\u2192\n    69\u2192Allows to set a custom port for the UWSGI server running in the container. While Docker containers have their own IP address namespace and port collisions are impossible to achieve, there are other container solutions that share one. Podman, for example, runs all containers in a pod under one namespace, which results in every port only being allowed to be assigned once. This option allows to set a custom port in order to avoid collisions with other containers.\n    70\u2192\n    71\u2192### `LD_CONTEXT_PATH`\n    72\u2192\n    73\u2192Values: `String` | Default = None\n    74\u2192\n    75\u2192Allows configuring the context path of the website. Useful for setting up Nginx reverse proxy.\n    76\u2192The context path must end with a slash. For example: `linkding/`\n    77\u2192\n    78\u2192### `LD_ENABLE_AUTH_PROXY`\n    79\u2192\n    80\u2192Values: `True`, `False` | Default = `False`\n    81\u2192\n    82\u2192Enables support for authentication proxies such as Authelia.\n    83\u2192This effectively disables credentials-based authentication and instead authenticates users if a specific request header contains a known username.\n    84\u2192You must make sure that your proxy (nginx, Traefik, Caddy, ...) forwards this header from your auth proxy to linkding. Check the documentation of your auth proxy and your reverse proxy on how to correctly set this up.\n    85\u2192\n    86\u2192Note that this automatically creates new users in the database if they do not already exist.\n    87\u2192\n    88\u2192Enabling this setting also requires configuring the following options:\n    89\u2192- `LD_AUTH_PROXY_USERNAME_HEADER` - The name of the request header that the auth proxy passes to the proxied application (linkding in this case), so that the application can identify the user.\n    90\u2192Check the documentation of your auth proxy to get this information.\n    91\u2192Note that the request headers are rewritten in linkding: all HTTP headers are prefixed with `HTTP_`, all letters are in uppercase, and dashes are replaced with underscores.\n    92\u2192For example, for Authelia, which passes the `Remote-User` HTTP header, the `LD_AUTH_PROXY_USERNAME_HEADER` needs to be configured as `HTTP_REMOTE_USER`.\n    93\u2192- `LD_AUTH_PROXY_LOGOUT_URL` - The URL that linkding should redirect to after a logout.\n    94\u2192By default, the logout redirects to the login URL, which means the user will be automatically authenticated again.\n    95\u2192Instead, you might want to configure the logout URL of the auth proxy here.\n    96\u2192\n    97\u2192### `LD_CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS`\n    98\u2192\n    99\u2192Values: `String` | Default = None\n   100\u2192\n   101\u2192List of trusted origins / host names to allow for `POST` requests, for example when logging in, or saving bookmarks.\n   102\u2192For these type of requests, the `Origin` header must match the `Host` header, otherwise the request will fail with a `403` status code, and the message `CSRF verification failed.`\n   103\u2192\n   104\u2192This option allows to declare a list of trusted origins that will be accepted even if the headers do not match. This can be the case when using a reverse proxy that rewrites the `Host` header, such as Nginx.\n   105\u2192\n   106\u2192For example, to allow requests to https://linkding.mydomain.com, configure the setting to `https://linkding.mydomain.com`.\n   107\u2192Note that the setting **must** include the correct protocol (`https` or `http`), and **must not** include the application / context path.\n   108\u2192Multiple origins can be specified by separating them with a comma (`,`).\n   109\u2192\n   110\u2192This setting is adopted from the Django framework used by linkding, more information on the setting is available in the [Django documentation](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/4.0/ref/settings/#std-setting-CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS).\n   111\u2192\n   112\u2192### `LD_LOG_X_FORWARDED_FOR`\n   113\u2192\n   114\u2192Values: `true` or `false` | Default =  `false`\n   115\u2192\n   116\u2192Set uWSGI [log-x-forwarded-for](https://uwsgi-docs.readthedocs.io/en/latest/Options.html?#log-x-forwarded-for) parameter allowing to keep the real IP of clients in logs when using a reverse proxy.\n   117\u2192\n   118\u2192### `LD_DB_ENGINE`\n   119\u2192\n   120\u2192Values: `postgres` or `sqlite` | Default = `sqlite`\n   121\u2192\n   122\u2192Database engine used by linkding to store data.\n   123\u2192Currently, linkding supports SQLite and PostgreSQL.\n   124\u2192By default, linkding uses SQLite, for which you don't need to configure anything.\n   125\u2192All the other database variables below are only required for configured PostgresSQL.\n   126\u2192\n   127\u2192### `LD_DB_DATABASE`\n   128\u2192\n   129\u2192Values: `String` | Default =  `linkding`\n   130\u2192\n   131\u2192The name of the database. \n   132\u2192\n   133\u2192### `LD_DB_USER`\n   134\u2192\n   135\u2192Values: `String` | Default =  `linkding`\n   136\u2192\n   137\u2192The name of the user to connect to the database server.\n   138\u2192\n   139\u2192### `LD_DB_PASSWORD`\n   140\u2192\n   141\u2192Values: `String` | Default =  None\n   142\u2192\n   143\u2192The password of the user to connect to the database server.\n   144\u2192The password must be configured when using a database other than SQLite, there is no default value.\n   145\u2192\n   146\u2192### `LD_DB_HOST`\n   147\u2192\n   148\u2192Values: `String` | Default =  `localhost`\n   149\u2192\n   150\u2192The hostname or IP of the database server.\n   151\u2192\n   152\u2192### `LD_DB_PORT`\n   153\u2192\n   154\u2192Values: `Integer` | Default =  None\n   155\u2192\n   156\u2192The port of the database server.\n   157\u2192Should use the default port if left empty, for example `5432` for PostgresSQL.\n   158\u2192\n   159\u2192### `LD_DB_OPTIONS`\n   160\u2192\n   161\u2192Values: `String` | Default = `{}`\n   162\u2192\n   163\u2192A json string with additional options for the database. Passed directly to OPTIONS.\n   164\u2192\n   165\u2192### `LD_FAVICON_PROVIDER`\n   166\u2192\n   167\u2192Values: `String` | Default =  `https://t1.gstatic.com/faviconV2?client=SOCIAL&type=FAVICON&fallback_opts=TYPE,SIZE,URL&url={url}&size=32`\n   168\u2192\n   169\u2192The favicon provider used for downloading icons if they are enabled in the user profile settings.\n   170\u2192The default provider is a Google service that automatically detects the correct